            <ArticleTitle>The Albury-Wodonga syndrome. A tale of two cities.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>135-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">An epidemic of acutely painful eyes having a seasonal restriction and an apparent response to topical indomethacin, is described.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">From patients seen in a rural private practice, the clinical picture of this acute syndrome and the use of indomethacin drops is described.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A favourable response to topical indomethacin in 21 patients is documented.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Topical indomethacin is worthy of further trial and investigation in the management of the Albury-Wodonga syndrome, harvester's eye, Christmas eye or seasonal corneal ulcer.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
